[Event "London System: the main line"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/ndKgjp3P/iz2eLzqV"]
[Role "teaching"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]

{ Welcome to the London System: your dark bishop goes out to f4 first, then a wall of three pawns grows in the middle behind it. Start with the queen pawn. [%cal Yd2d4] }
1. d4 { Your pawn takes a share of the middle and opens the road for your dark bishop. [%cal Gc1f4] } (1. e4 { The king pawn is every bit as good, but it leads to other openings. } 1... e5 2. Nf3 { Good chess, and the road to the Ruy Lopez. The London starts with the queen pawn. [%csl Be4] }) (1. Nf3 { A flexible start, and the London can still follow. } 1... d5 2. d4 { Level: with d4 in, your bishop can still come to f4 next. [%cal Bc1f4] }) 1... Nf6 { His knight comes out and watches e4. Against d5 you would play the same moves. Now bring your dark bishop out at once. [%cal Yc1f4,Rf6e4] } 2. Bf4 { That is the London bishop: out before your e pawn moves, so the wall you build next never shuts it in. [%cal Gf4b8] } (2. c4 { Very good too, but that is a different family of openings. } 2... e6 3. Nf3 { Level and sound: a queen gambit style game, another study. The London keeps the c pawn at home. [%csl Bc4] }) (2. Nf3 { Just as good: many London players bring this knight out first. } 2... d5 3. Bf4 { The same London, knight first and bishop second. Level. [%cal Gc1f4] }) (2. e3 { Natural, but now the pawn on e3 blocks your dark bishop. } 2... e6 3. Nf3 { Level, but your bishop on c1 is shut in behind your own pawn, and f4 is out of its reach. [%csl Rc1] }) 2... d5 { He takes his share of the middle. Against e6 the same moves follow. Now the first stone of the wall: pawn to e3. [%cal Ye2e3] } 3. e3 { The e pawn guards d4 and opens a road for your light bishop. [%cal Ge3d4,Gf1d3] } (3. Nf3 { Just as good: the knight first, and e3 comes next. } 3... c5 { Level: the same London by another road, with e3 still to come. [%csl Bf3] }) (3. Nd2 { Just as good: the queen knight first, and e3 still to come. } 3... c5 4. e3 { Level: the same London, the knight a move early. [%csl Bd2] }) (3. Nc3 { The knight to c3 is playable, but it stands where your c pawn wants to go. } 3... c5 4. e3 { Level, but the c3 square is taken, so the wall cannot be finished. [%csl Rc3] }) 3... c5 { He hits your d pawn with his c pawn. Against e6 you play the same moves; against a bishop move, your light bishop offers a trade. Next stone: your c pawn one step, so your d pawn keeps a pawn guard. [%cal Yc2c3] } 4. c3 { Now the wall stands: your d pawn has a pawn guard on each side, and your bishop is outside it, on f4. [%cal Gc3d4,Ge3d4] } (4. Nf3 { Just as good: the knight comes out, and c3 can follow. } 4... Nc6 5. Nbd2 { Level: the same pieces, and the pawn to c3 still to come. [%csl Bc3] }) (4. Nd2 { Also good: the queen knight first. } 4... Nc6 5. Ngf3 { Level: both knights are out, and c3 can come later. [%csl Bc3] }) (4. Nc3 { Developing the knight here blocks the c pawn for good. } 4... cxd4 5. exd4 { Level, but with the knight on c3 your c pawn cannot join the wall. [%csl Rc3] }) (4. dxc5 { Taking on c5 is sharper, and you have to hold the pawn with b4. } 4... e6 5. b4 a5 6. c3 { Level: a pawn up for now, but his a pawn is already chipping at your queen side. The wall is simpler. [%cal Ra5b4] }) 4... Nc6 { His knight adds pressure to your d pawn. Against a queen move, your queen meets hers. Now your queen knight to d2, since your c pawn already holds c3. [%cal Yb1d2] } 5. Nd2 { The knight guards e4, and it leaves f3 free for your other knight. [%cal Gd2e4,Gg1f3] } (5. Nf3 { Just as good: the king knight first. } 5... Nh5 6. Bg3 Nxg3 7. hxg3 { Level: his knight took your bishop, and the h column opened for your rook. [%cal Gh1h7] }) (5. Bd3 { The bishop comes out first, but b2 is left without a guard. } 5... Qb6 6. b3 { About level, but his queen hit b2 at once and you spent a move guarding it. [%csl Rb2] }) 5... e6 { He opens a road for his dark bishop. Against a bishop move, your queen comes out to hit his b pawn; if he takes on d4, your e pawn takes back. Now the other knight comes out. [%cal Yg1f3] } 6. Ngf3 { Both knights are out, one watching e5 and the other e4. [%cal Gf3e5,Gd2e4] } (6. Bd3 { Also fine, and very common: the bishop first. } 6... Bd6 7. Bg3 { Level: the same set-up in another order. [%csl Bd3] }) (6. Be2 { Playable, but on e2 the bishop aims at nothing; d3 is its better square. } 6... Bd6 7. Bg3 { Level: the same set-up, only with your bishop on the quieter e2 square. [%csl Be2] }) 6... Bd6 { His bishop offers a trade. Against bishop e7, or c takes d4, you build on the same way. Here your bishop steps back: if he takes, your h pawn takes back and opens the h column. [%cal Yf4g3,Rd6f4] } 7. Bg3 { The bishop keeps its diagonal and faces his bishop on d6. Trading is his choice now, and it would open your h column. [%cal Gg3d6,Gh1h7] } (7. Bxd6 { Trading at once is playable, but it helps his queen out. } 7... Qxd6 8. Bb5 { Level: his queen took back and now stands in the middle of his camp. [%csl Rd6] }) (7. Ne5 { Jumping into e5 this early leaves the knight without support. } 7... Qc7 8. Ndf3 c4 { About level, but his queen and pawns push you around while your king is still in the middle. [%csl Re1] }) (7. Bb5 { Pinning his knight is playable, but he trades bishops on f4. } 7... Bxf4 8. exf4 { Level: the bishops are gone, and your e pawn now stands on f4. [%csl Bf4] }) 7... O-O { He castles. The last piece of the set-up: your light bishop to d3, aiming at h7 beside his king. [%cal Yf1d3,Gd3h7] } 8. Bd3 { That is the London System: a wall of three pawns in the middle, your dark bishop outside it, and your light bishop aiming at his king's side. [%csl Gc3,Gd4,Ge3][%cal Gg3d6,Gd3h7] } (8. Bb5 { Just as good: the bishop pins his knight instead. } 8... Ne7 9. Bd3 { Level: his knight left c6, and your bishop settles on d3 after all. [%csl Bd3] }) (8. Ne5 { Also good: the knight jumps in at once. } 8... cxd4 9. exd4 { Level: the knight sits on e5, and his c pawn has opened the c column. [%csl Be5] }) (8. Be2 { Safe, but on e2 the bishop no longer aims at his king. } 8... b6 9. O-O { Level, but on e2 your bishop has no view of his king. [%csl Re2] }) 1-0


[Event "London System: he hits d4 at once"]
