{ Your bishop is out on f4, and your e pawn has not moved yet. }
2... c5 { He hits d4 with his c pawn before anything else. You could guard your pawn, or take his. Is there something better? [%csl Bd4] } 3. d5 { Right: push past him. Your pawn on d5 grabs space, and his pawn on c5 now bites on nothing. [%csl Gd5,Rc5] } (3. e3 { Solid, and the most played, but his queen comes to b6 at once. } 3... Qb6 4. Nc3 { Level: a fine game, but his queen already hits b2, and d5 would have gained more. [%cal Rb6b2] }) (3. c3 { Guarding d4 with a pawn is playable but slow. [%cal Bc3d4] } 3... Qb6 4. Qc1 { Level, but your queen is stuck at home guarding b2 while his is active. [%cal Rc1b2] }) (3. dxc5 { Taking is playable, and he wins the pawn back. } 3... Na6 4. f3 Nxc5 5. e4 { Also good: he got the pawn back, and your pawns grabbed the middle. [%csl Ge4] }) (3. Nf3 { Developing lets him trade on d4 and hit your queen. } 3... cxd4 4. Qxd4 Nc6 { About level, but your queen is chased at once, and his pawn trade opened the c column. [%cal Rc6d4] }) (3. Nd2 { The London knight move, but here it blocks your queen's guard of d4. [%cal Rd1d4] } 3... cxd4 { A pawn down: his pawn took d4, and nothing of yours can take back. [%csl Rd4] }) (3. Nc3 { The knight comes out, but he trades on d4, and your queen has to take back. } 3... cxd4 4. Qxd4 Nc6 { About level, but his knight comes out with a hit on your queen, and she must move again. [%cal Rc6d4] }) 3... d6 { He guards his c pawn and gets ready to chip at your pawn on d5. Against b5, bring out your king knight; against a queen move, your queen knight, and if she grabs your b pawn, your bishop drops back to guard it. } 4. Nc3 { Right: the knight backs up d5 and is ready to support e4. [%cal Gc3d5,Gc3e4] } (4. Nf3 { Natural, but it does nothing for d5. } 4... e5 5. dxe6 Bxe6 { Level: his e pawn swapped off your pawn on d5, and his bishop came out with it. [%csl Re6] }) (4. c4 { Guarding d5 with a pawn lets his e pawn arrive with a hit on your bishop. [%cal Bc4d5] } 4... e5 5. Bg5 h6 6. Bxf6 Qxf6 { About level, but you had to give your bishop for his knight, and his queen is out. [%csl Rf6] }) (4. e3 { The e pawn steps up and leaves b2 without a guard. } 4... Qb6 5. b3 { He is better: his queen hit b2 at once, and your queen side is loose. [%csl Rb3,Rc3] }) (4. Nd2 { The knight on d2 blocks your queen's guard of d5. [%cal Rd1d5] } 4... Nxd5 5. Bg3 { A pawn down: his knight took d5 and hit your bishop on the way. [%csl Rd5] }) (4. e4 { Too soon: nothing guards e4 yet. [%csl Re4] } 4... Nxe4 5. Bd3 Nf6 { Slightly better for him: you are a pawn down, with only a little play for it. [%csl Re4] }) 4... g6 { He gets ready to put his bishop behind his g pawn. Had he pushed e5, your bishop would step forward to pin his knight; against a6, your a pawn steps up to stop his b pawn. [%cal Rf8g7] } 5. e4 { Two pawns in the middle against his one, and his c pawn still bites on nothing. That is what pushing past gives you. [%csl Gd5,Ge4,Rd6,Rc5] } (5. Nf3 { Just as good: the knight first, and e4 comes next. } 5... Bg7 6. e4 { The same big centre, a move later, and just as good. [%csl Ge4] }) (5. a4 { Just as good: your a pawn keeps his b pawn at home. } 5... Bg7 6. e4 { The same centre, with a4 thrown in. Slightly better for you. [%csl Ga4] }) (5. Qd2 { A little slow: his b pawn jumps forward at once. } 5... b5 6. Nxb5 Ne4 { About level: you took a pawn, but his knight hits your queen and his pieces come alive. [%cal Re4d2] }) (5. e3 { The London pawn move, but here it cuts your bishop off from d2. [%cal Rf4d2] } 5... Qa5 { His queen pins your knight to your king. [%cal Ra5e1] } 6. Qd2 Bg7 { Slightly better for him: your queen had to step to d2 to hold the pinned knight. [%csl Rc3] }) 1-0


