[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkb1r/pp2pppp/2n2n2/2pp4/3P1B2/2P1P3/PP1N1PPP/R2QKBNR_b_KQkq_-_2_5"]

{ Your wall is up and your bishop is out on f4, which means nothing guards the pawn on b2 any more. [%csl Rb2] }
5... Qb6 { His queen comes out and hits b2, and his c pawn adds pressure to d4. How do you meet her? [%cal Rb6b2] } 6. Qb3 { Right: queen meets queen. She guards b2, and if he trades, your a pawn takes back and your rook gets the a column. [%cal Gb3b6,Gb3b2] } (6. Qc2 { The queen guards b2 from c2, but his knight hunts your bishop. [%cal Bc2b2] } 6... Nh5 7. Be5 { About level: his knight made your bishop step to e5, and his pieces are free. [%csl Re5] }) (6. Qc1 { Guarding b2 from the back row is passive. [%cal Bc1b2] } 6... cxd4 7. exd4 e5 { About level, but he opened the middle while your queen sat on c1. [%csl Re5] }) (6. Ngf3 { Developing is natural, but b2 is simply loose. } 6... Qxb2 7. Rb1 Qxc3 { He took two pawns on the queen side, and your quick pieces are not quite enough for them. Slightly better for him. [%csl Rb2,Rc3] }) (6. Bd3 { The bishop comes out, and b2 falls. } 6... Qxb2 7. Ne2 { A pawn down, with his queen deep in your camp. Slightly better for him. [%csl Rb2] }) (6. b3 { The pawn steps off b2, but it spends a move while he opens the middle. } 6... cxd4 7. exd4 e5 { Slightly better for him: his e pawn hits your bishop and your d pawn at once. [%cal Re5f4,Re5d4] }) (6. Rb1 { The rook guards b2, but it stands where his bishop can hit it. [%cal Bb1b2] } 6... cxd4 7. exd4 Bf5 { His bishop hits your rook on b1. [%cal Rf5b1] } 8. Rc1 Qxb2 { A pawn down: your rook had to leave b1, and b2 fell anyway. [%csl Rb2] }) 6... c4 { His pawn hits your queen. Had his bishop come to f5, your d pawn would take on c5. Where does your queen go now? [%cal Rc4b3] } 7. Qc2 { Right: from c2 your queen still guards b2 along the row. [%cal Gc2b2] } (7. Qxb6 { Trading queens opens the a column for his rook. } 7... axb6 8. a3 b5 { Slightly better for him: his rook owns the a column, and his b pawns march. [%cal Ra8a3] }) (7. Nxc4 { The knight grabs c4, but his d pawn takes it back and your queen is attacked again. } 7... dxc4 8. Bxc4 e6 { A knight for two pawns: he is better. [%csl Rc4] }) (7. Qd1 { Back home leaves b2 hanging. } 7... Qxb2 { His queen simply takes b2 and sits in your camp. [%csl Rb2] }) (7. Qa3 { From a3 the queen still guards b2, but she stands on his bishop's diagonal. [%cal Ba3b2] } 7... e5 { His pawn hits your bishop and opens his bishop's road to your queen at once. [%cal Re5f4,Rf8a3] } 8. Qa4 exf4 { A bishop down: one pawn push hit two of your pieces. [%csl Rf4] }) 7... g6 { He gets ready to put his bishop behind his g pawn. Against bishop g4 your knight would come out to f3, and against a knight move to the edge, your bishop steps forward. A quiet move lets him finish in peace. } 8. e4 { There it is: your e pawn hits d5, the pawn that holds his c pawn up, while his queen is far away. [%cal Ge4d5,Rd5c4] } (8. h3 { Calm, and he finishes his set-up. } 8... Bg7 9. Ngf3 { Level: nothing wrong, but his pieces are all out. [%csl Bg7] }) (8. Ngf3 { Developing lets his bishop come out with a hit. } 8... Bf5 9. Qc1 { About level, but his bishop drove your queen back to c1. [%cal Rf5c2] }) (8. b3 { Hitting his chain from the side is too slow here. } 8... Bf5 9. Qd1 { Slightly better for him: his bishop chased your queen home. [%csl Rd1] }) (8. Be2 { Calm development, but his bishop comes out with a hit on your queen. } 8... Bf5 9. Qc1 { Level: your queen stepped back to c1, and his pieces come out in comfort. [%csl Rc1] }) 8... Nxe4 { His knight grabs the pawn. Had his pawn taken on e4, your knight would win back his c pawn; against e6, your pawn would jump forward and kick his knight. Take the knight back. } 9. Nxe4 { Knight for knight. If his d pawn takes back, his pawn on c4 loses its guard. [%csl Rc4] } 9... dxe4 { His pawn takes, and his pawn on c4 now stands alone. } 10. Bxc4 { The pawn is back, and your bishop looks straight at f7. [%cal Gc4f7] } (10. d5 { Pushing on is playable, and the pawns come off. } 10... Na5 11. Be3 Qc7 12. Qxe4 { Level: material is even, a quieter game than the main line. [%csl Be4] }) 10... Bg7 { His bishop comes to g7 rather than f5, and now your d pawn is hit three times: by the bishop, the knight and his queen. [%cal Rg7d4,Rc6d4,Rb6d4] } 11. Ne2 { The knight guards d4 a third time. Material is level, and his e pawn stands cut off from his army. Against bishop f5, the same knight move works. [%cal Ge2d4][%csl Re4] } (11. Be3 { Just as good: the bishop guards d4 instead. [%cal Be3d4] } 11... Na5 12. Be2 { As good as the main line: his knight chased your bishop back to e2, but d4 holds. [%csl Gd4] }) (11. Qb3 { Offering a queen trade keeps d4 safe, but it is tame. } 11... Qxb3 12. axb3 { Level: the queens are gone, and so is most of your pressure. [%csl Bb3] }) (11. Rd1 { The rook guards d4, but it is the slowest guard. [%cal Bd1d4] } 11... O-O 12. Ne2 { Level: d4 holds, but your king is still in the middle. [%csl Re1] }) 1-0


