{ Your bishop has stepped back to g3, and it faces his bishop on d6. }
7... Bxg3 { He takes your bishop on g3. Two pawns can take back. Which one? } 8. hxg3 { Right: the h pawn takes, and the h column opens for your rook. Your g pawns are doubled, but they guard f4 and h4. [%cal Gh1h7][%csl Gf4,Gh4] } (8. fxg3 { The f pawn takes, but your e3 pawn loses its guard. } 8... Ng4 9. Qe2 Qc7 { Slightly better for him: his knight on g4 hits e3, and your king side has holes. [%cal Rg4e3] }) 8... Qd6 { His queen takes the diagonal your bishop left. Neither king has castled yet. } 9. Bb5 { Your bishop pins his knight on c6 to his king, and your rook on h1 looks straight up the open column. [%cal Gb5e8,Gh1h7] } (9. Bd3 { Just as good: the bishop aims at h7 beside the open column. } 9... e5 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 11. dxe5 Qxe5 { Level: his e5 break swapped off two pairs of pieces, and material is even. [%csl Be5] }) (9. Qc2 { Also good: the queen comes out first. } 9... Bd7 10. Bb5 { Level: the same pin, a move later. [%csl Bb5] }) (9. Rh4 { A lively idea: the rook swings along the fourth row. } 9... h6 10. Qe2 { Level: the rook stands active, but your king is still in the middle. [%csl Bh4] }) (9. Be2 { Just as good: the bishop comes out quietly, and you can castle. } 9... O-O 10. O-O { Level: both kings are safe, and the game is quiet. [%csl Gg1] }) 1-0


