{ Your London bishop is out on f4 again, and this time he answers in another way. }
2... g6 { He pushes his g pawn to put his bishop behind it on g7. That is a fianchetto: a bishop placed on the long diagonal. Keep the London calm: e3 first. [%cal Ye2e3,Ra1h8] } 3. e3 { The e pawn guards d4. Against the fianchetto you keep the set-up quiet and flexible. [%cal Ge3d4] } (3. Nc3 { Just as good, and a livelier way to play. } 3... d5 4. e3 { Level: a sharp set-up of its own, with the knight blocking your c pawn. This study keeps the London calm. [%csl Bc3] }) (3. Nf3 { Just as good: the knight first. } 3... Bg7 4. e3 { The same set-up, knight first. Level. [%csl Bf3] }) (3. c4 { Good, but that is another opening, with c4. } 3... c5 4. d5 { Level and sharp: a different kind of game. [%csl Bc4] }) 3... Bg7 { His bishop lands on g7, behind his knight. Bring your king knight to f3. [%cal Yg1f3] } 4. Nf3 { The knight guards d4 and e5 and gets your king ready to castle. [%cal Gf3d4,Gf3e5] } (4. h3 { Just as good: h3 first, and the knight next. } 4... O-O { Level: he castles, and your knight still comes to f3. h3 and Nf3 simply swap places. [%csl Bh3] }) (4. Be2 { Just as good: the bishop first. } 4... O-O { Level: he castles, and your knight follows to f3 with the same set-up. [%csl Be2] }) 4... O-O { He castles. Against d6 first, the same moves follow. Your light bishop goes to e2, a quiet square. [%cal Yf1e2] } 5. Be2 { The bishop is safe on e2, out of reach of his pawns, and your king is one step from castling. [%csl Ge2] } (5. h3 { Just as good: h3 before the bishop. } 5... d6 6. Be2 { Level: the same position by another road. [%csl Bh3] }) (5. Nbd2 { Also good: the knight comes to d2 before the bishop moves. } 5... d6 6. c3 { Level: a solid set-up with c3, though your king is a move further from castling. [%csl Bd2] }) (5. Bd3 { Also good, though on d3 the bishop stands in reach of his e pawn. } 5... d6 6. O-O { Level, but your bishop and knight now stand side by side, where one pawn of his on e4 would hit both. [%cal Re4d3,Re4f3] }) 5... d6 { He props his centre, ready to push his e pawn later. Against c5 you keep the same plan. Now a little pawn move: h3, which makes a home for your bishop behind it. [%cal Yh2h3,Gf4h2] } 6. h3 { Now if his knight jumps to h5 to hunt your bishop, it steps back to h2 and stays on its diagonal. [%cal Gf4h2] } (6. O-O { Just as good, but after knight h5 your bishop has to leave its diagonal. } 6... Nh5 7. Bg5 { Level, but your bishop had to go to g5; the little pawn move first would have kept it on its diagonal. [%csl Rg5] }) (6. Nbd2 { The knight comes out, and his knight goes hunting. } 6... Nh5 7. Bg5 h6 8. Bh4 { About level, but your bishop is being chased around the board. [%csl Rh4] }) 6... Nbd7 { His knight comes out to back up his e pawn push. Against c5 or b6 you castle all the same. Tuck your king away. [%cal Ye1g1] } 7. O-O { That is the London against the fianchetto: two pawns in the middle, knight and bishop out, king castled, and a safe square ready behind your bishop. [%csl Gg1,Gh2] } (7. Bh2 { Just as good: the bishop steps back before he even asks. } 7... b6 8. O-O { Level: the same set-up, bishop already on h2. [%csl Gh2] }) (7. c4 { Grabbing space before castling lets his e5 push come first. } 7... e5 8. Bh2 { About level, but his e5 came while your king was still in the middle. [%csl Re1] }) (7. Nbd2 { Just as good: the knight comes out first. } 7... a5 8. O-O { Level: castling a move later changes little. [%csl Bd2] }) 1-0


